Document 0 Product brief
Product name
LedgerLens
One-line
Local-first finance intake that parses messy bank statements and receipts, auto-categorizes transactions, learns from corrections, and supports item-level bill splitting.
Platforms


Android APK


Desktop local app (Windows first, then macOS, then Linux)


Core problems


Bank exports are inconsistent, hard to parse, and often PDF-only.


Categorization is tedious and requires constant manual fixes.


Group expenses require item-level assignment and tax allocation, which current apps make painful.


Users want “hands-off” budgeting, but full automation requires expensive integrations and high trust.


Strategy


Phase-gated capability ladder:


Local ingestion + parsing + categorization + learning loop


Local budgeting and insights + optional cloud sync


Read-only bank API aggregation


Assisted actions (draft bill pay/transfers)


Fully automated actions only after safety, permissions, and compliance gates




Key principles


Local-first by default: no account/PII required to get value.


Deterministic fallbacks: rules + templates + human-in-the-loop, not “LLM hallucination.”


Auditability: every categorization and split has an explanation trail.


Progressive trust: write-access automation is opt-in and gated.



Document 1 Product requirements
Goals


Ingest bank statements (PDF/CSV) from any bank and normalize into a canonical transaction ledger.


Categorize transactions with high precision, improving over time from user corrections.


Parse receipts/bills into line items, then split costs by participant at item level, allocating tax/fees correctly.


Operate fully offline (local machine / device) for MVP.


Provide a clean migration path from localhost-only to cloud-enabled sync and compute.


Non-goals for MVP


Direct bank login credential storage.


Bill pay execution, ACH initiation, investment trades.


Real-time bank transaction streaming.


Guaranteed-perfect OCR on low-quality scans without user review.


Primary personas


Solo budgeter: wants automated categorization and monthly summaries.


Household manager: wants to reconcile, tag, and split shared purchases.


Roommates/group: wants receipt splits and settlements with minimal friction.


Power user: wants import/export, rules, and audit logs.


User journeys
Journey A Bank statement import to categorized ledger


User imports PDF/CSV


App detects format, extracts transactions


App deduplicates and normalizes


App assigns categories with confidence scores and explanations


User reviews “low confidence” items, corrects categories


App learns and updates future predictions


Journey B Receipt to item-level split with tax allocation


User uploads receipt image/PDF (or a “receipt-style list” exported from email)


OCR + parser extracts merchant, date, items, quantities, line prices, tax, tip, fees


App prompts to add/select participants


User assigns each item (or fractions) to participants


App computes per-person subtotal, proportional tax/tip/fees, and net owed to payer(s)


App generates settlement summary and export/shareable record


Journey C Budget insights


App aggregates by month, category, merchant


Shows anomalies, recurring charges, subscription detection


User sets lightweight targets


App flags deviations and upcoming known bills


Functional scope
Import sources


PDF bank statements (text-based and scanned)


CSV exports (bank-provided variations)


Optional later: OFX/QIF


Receipt images/PDFs (for splitting and optional posting as transactions)


